  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1004 PM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nWith moisture still plentiful and an afternoon sounding \nbolstering the most CAPE we've seen in quite some time (nearly \n2000 J/kg), today saw, and continues to see, heightened storm \nactivity. High rainfall rates have prompted numerous flash \nfloods and powerful storms have dropped severe winds and small \nhail. As we progress through the overnight hours, a mixture of \nstratiform rain showers and clusters of stronger convection are \nexpected to continue. While the wind and hail threat should \ndiminish with lessened instability, rain rates could continue to\nbe a concern and a few additional flash floods or debris flows \ncannot be ruled out.\n\nEXTREMELY MOIST WEDNESDAY AND THURSDAY:\n\nBelieve it or not, we will transition into an even wetter \nenvironment over the next 48 hours as yet another surge of sub-\ntropical moisture pushes into the Intermountain West through \nthe persistent southwesterly flow. Ensembles are keying in on \nPWAT's nearing 300% of normal, particularly focusing on the \nsouthwestern corner of Colorado. At this magnitude of an \nanomaly, PWAT's are not just forecasted to break records, but to\ncrush them.\n\nWith that much moisture, it's almost a certainty that cloud \ncover will be widespread for the vast majority of the next 48 \nhours. This is the one factor dampening storm potential, and \nCAM's are responding by depicting a stratiform storm mode \ndominating the region. While we often talk about stratiform \nrainfall being less threatening to flash flooding concerns due \nto weaker rainfall rates, the Wednesday/Thursday event isn't \nquite in the same boat. For one, the low-level environment and \nsoils will have both sufficiently saturated by this point thanks\nto many portions of the region seeing 1+ inches of rainfall \nover the past couple days. This will lead to efficient rainfall \nand minimal absorption by said soils. We've already seen some \nvulnerable locations produce flash flooding and debris flows \nwith weaker rainfall rates, an indication that there is little \nroom left for the land to take on more water. Secondly, just \nbecause stratiform storms will dominate does not mean embedded \nstronger cells cannot develop. Between upper-level jet support, \na pronounced shortwave passing through (roughly between 6 PM \nlocal time Wednesday through 6 PM local time Thursday), and \nenhanced PVA with the southward digging of the west coast low, \nthe dynamics are in place for a handful of strong updrafts and \nespecially wet rainfall rates. Even so, it is likely that we \nwill see less of the hail and wind threat over the next couple \ndays, and more of the hydrologic threats. Refer to the hydro \nsection below for more detailed information on what to expect.\n\nFRIDAY AND BEYOND:\n\nOn Friday, the moisture pool driving this wet weather rapidly \ndiminishes. Though, given just how wet we are expected to peak\nat, even with this decrease in moisture we are confident that \nthe majority of the region will still see above normal PWAT's \nthrough the weekend. With the low beginning its eastwards \nmovement though, and with little in the way of forcing, storm \nactivity should become less pronounced. On Friday, there looks\nto still be enough moisture to keep storm coverage scattered. \nBy Sunday though, look for most shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment to be confined to the high terrain as convection \nwill be driven by orographics.",
